WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.220
If raising TrkA is not enough, what else must NGF change?

2
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.640
NGF also controls how long its own signal lasts.

3
00:00:13.383 --> 00:00:17.123
NGF is caught at the axon tip, far from the cell body.

4
00:00:18.043 --> 00:00:20.583
Yet it changes which genes the nucleus turns on.

5
00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:24.493
So the signal has to travel back up the axon.

6
00:00:25.193 --> 00:00:27.093
Much of it travels inside endosomes.

7
00:00:28.117 --> 00:00:34.217
One protein that NGF induces, Coronin-1, keeps these signaling endosomes active

8
00:00:34.217 --> 00:00:35.217
longer.

9
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.580
It is required for normal competition in the mouse.

10
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.660
It is one mechanism, and there may be others.

11
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:44.590
Now test the idea in a model.

12
00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:50.290
The computational neuroscientists Stefan Mihalas and Ernst Niebur joined the group.

13
00:00:51.283 --> 00:00:56.663
They simulated neurons competing for NGF that the target makes at a constant rate.

14
00:00:57.667 --> 00:00:59.227
They compared four conditions.

15
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:03.487
No feedback. Feedback on TrkA level alone.

16
00:01:04.517 --> 00:01:06.637
Feedback on signal duration alone.

17
00:01:07.277 --> 00:01:08.557
And feedback on both.

18
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:13.290
Which of the four conditions split the neurons into winners and losers?

19
00:01:19.883 --> 00:01:21.303
Only feedback on both.

20
00:01:22.183 --> 00:01:24.323
With either one alone, competition failed.

21
00:01:25.383 --> 00:01:30.063
The two-neuron arithmetic gives a reason for both failures, at least in a toy

22
00:01:30.063 --> 00:01:31.063
version.

23
00:01:31.466 --> 00:01:36.286
Level alone, with capture in proportion to receptor, keeps every ratio fixed.

24
00:01:37.406 --> 00:01:40.606
Duration alone never changes the catch, so the shares stay put.

25
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.080
Level and duration together close a loop.

26
00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:49.300
A signal that rises faster than the lead feeds the next catch, so in this toy the

27
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.300
lead grows.

28
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.660
This toy reason is not the published equations.

29
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.140
It only illustrates why one loop was not enough.

30
00:01:58.183 --> 00:02:02.823
In the published simulations, the two loops together separated winners from losers.

31
00:02:03.983 --> 00:02:06.503
The lab described the sequence as four stages.

32
00:02:07.566 --> 00:02:11.486
Naive. A young neuron responds poorly to NGF.

33
00:02:12.566 --> 00:02:16.906
Sensitization. It gets enough NGF to cross a threshold.

34
00:02:17.906 --> 00:02:21.066
From then on, NGF makes it more sensitive to NGF.

35
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.460
Competitive advantage.

36
00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.880
It raises TrkA and strengthens the signaling downstream of it.

37
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.500
Selection. It wins the supply, and neighbours that stayed behind lose.

38
00:02:34.583 --> 00:02:36.343
One more piece speeds the split.

39
00:02:36.343 --> 00:02:39.383
The next chapter asks how winners treat the losers.

40
00:02:40.516 --> 00:02:45.876
Takeaway. NGF raises both receptor level and signal duration.

41
00:02:46.836 --> 00:02:49.856
In the published model, only the two together split the neurons.
